  "text": "None of the three solutions is reliably correct. Solution 2 is clearly wrong, and Solutions 1 and 3 both rest on a value I believe is incorrect.\n\n**Solution 2** asserts |s^t(S_n)| = C_{n-t} with no derivation. It fails at the first case: |s(S_n)| is not a Catalan number. For n = 3 the image is {123, 213}, size 2 = C_2, but for n = 5 the image size is 17, while C_4 = 14. Its answer also gives 42 − 52 = −10 at m = 5, which is implausible for the problem's intended answer.\n\n**Solutions 1 and 3** both use |s(S_6)| = 68 as the m = 5 base case. I recall the sorted-permutation counts as 1, 1, 2, 5, 17, 70, 349, 2017 for n = 1, …, 8. That matches the small cases I checked (|s(S_3)| = 2, |s(S_4)| = 5, and |s(S_5)| = 17 fits the threshold pattern). If that memory is right, |s(S_6)| = 70, not 68, so the m = 5 difference would be 18, and neither (m−1)² nor (m²+7m−28)/2 gives 18. I could not verify the value 70 by hand, so this is a recollection, not a checked result.\n\nThe two formulas also disagree at m = 7 (36 versus 35), and neither has been independently confirmed. Solution 1's (m−1)² is a fit to three points. Solution 3's recurrence is only checked for t = 2, 3, and its closed form fails at m = 4, which may explain the problem's m ≥ 5 restriction.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
